Message-ID: <CAPhhD8nUvmHid7Ywzx+tPcOKTwTeGk8iGJyzRMuQDMvE7ccRQw@mail.gmail.com>

Hi Yanyi,

I'm not an author of *MatchIt* but I know it well. There are three issues
I've seen with *MatchIt* and categorical (i.e., factor) variables.

First, when using distance = "mahalanobis", factor variables are not
allowed. You need to split them into dummy (0/1) variables. My *cobalt*
package has a function, splitfactor(), that will split each factor in your
dataset into dummies. Run data_ <- splitfactor(data, drop.first = TRUE) and
then use data_ in matchit() (you'll need to adjust the formula too). Do not
use the numeric version of a factor anywhere in matchit().

When using any other distance, factor variables will be processed correctly
for the distance estimation and matching. They will not be processed
correctly for the balance output. Only the first factor in your formula
will have balance displayed for all levels when you use summary(). To get
around this and see balance on all factor levels for every factor, you can
use bal.tab() in the *cobalt* package, which doesn't suffer from this
issue. (Make sure you read the *cobalt* documentation carefully to know
what values you're getting; they will be the same as those from *MatchIt*
if you set some options to certain values.)

Finally, the treatment variable cannot be a factor. It must be a numeric or
logical variable.

Otherwise, I haven't seen any problems with using *MatchIt* with factor
variables. For most cases (i.e., for propensity score matching), they will
work without a hitch. For balance assessment, *cobalt* is your best bet.

Noah

